  data: {
    LLM: "Conserve your high cards for high prizes. Bidding a Jack for a 2 of Diamonds is almost always a mistake, as you leave yourself vulnerable when the King of Diamonds eventually appears.\n\nThe 'N+1' Rule: Try to beat your opponent's likely bid by exactly one point. If you think they will play a 7 for an 8-prize, play an 8. Winning a trick by 1 point is the hallmark of efficient GOPS play.\n\nTie Manipulation: If a low-value prize is up (like a 3), and you suspect a tie will happen, you might deliberately play a very low card (like an Ace) to 'save' a midndtier card, knowing that you can fight for the rolledndover prize in the next round.",
    Player: "Conserve your high cards for high prizes. Bidding a Jack for a 2 of Diamonds is almost always a mistake, as you leave yourself vulnerable when the King of Diamonds eventually appears.\n\nThe 'N+1' Rule: Try to beat your opponent's likely bid by exactly one point. If you think they will play a 7 for an 8-prize, play an 8. Winning a trick by 1 point is the hallmark of efficient GOPS play.\n\nTie Manipulation: If a low-value prize is up (like a 3), and you suspect a tie will happen, you might deliberately play a very low card (like an Ace) to 'save' a midndtier card, knowing that you can fight for the rolledndover prize in the next round.",
    aggressiveness: 0.5,
    riskTolerance: 0.5,
    bluffFrequency: 0.3,
    bluffSettings: {
      profile: 'balanced',
    },
    basic: 'Conserve your high cards for high prizes. Bidding a Jack for a 2 of Diamonds is almost always a mistake, as you leave yourself vulnerable when the King of Diamonds eventually appears.',
    intermediate: "The 'N+1' Rule: Try to beat your opponent's likely bid by exactly one point. If you think they will play a 7 for an 8-prize, play an 8. Winning a trick by 1 point is the hallmark of efficient GOPS play.",
    advanced: "Tie Manipulation: If a low-value prize is up (like a 3), and you suspect a tie will happen, you might deliberately play a very low card (like an Ace) to 'save' a midndtier card, knowing that you can fight for the rolledndover prize in the next round.",
    tips: [
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: 'Pay attention to what cards your opponent has already used. If they have already played their King and Queen, and you still have yours, you are in a dominant position for the final rounds.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Tip',
        description: "If you cannot win a prize, play your lowest possible card (the 'trash card' strategy) to minimize your loss of bidding power.",
      },
    ],
